#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""freecode — opencode on local models only.

  freecode [opencode arguments]   checks the configuration and starts opencode (TUI or `run`)
  freecode --configure [STAGES]   configuration in 4 stages (like bootstrap; every change asks first):
                                    1 connection to a local model, 2 conformity with the template,
                                    3 the model's work style (a/b/c), 4 computer description in AGENTS.md
                                  e.g. `--configure 3` = work style only; the first start does 1 and 4 itself
  freecode --set KEY=a ...        change the work style without questions (keys: options narration risk language)
  freecode --undo                 undo the last work-style change (a second time = redo)
  freecode --check                configuration check only (exit 0 = OK to start)
  freecode --env                  refresh the computer description in AGENTS.md (system, GPU, model)
  freecode --version              version of freecode and the opencode underneath

freecode is a WRAPPER around unmodified opencode, not a fork: opencode updates normally
(npm i -g @opencode/cli), and on every start freecode makes sure it runs on local models only.

Why: opencode 2.x without a correct configuration (missing file, broken JSON, no local
`small_model`) silently sends the conversation to free cloud models. Before every start freecode
checks all configuration sources and refuses to start if any provider is not local.
It also refuses on `share: auto`, a non-local `enterprise.url`, a remote MCP server and an overridden model address.
It does not pass to opencode commands that go out to the network or expose a server: `upgrade`, `auth`, `acp`,
`service`, `uninstall`, `plugin add/check/update`, `mcp auth`, `pair --remote`, non-local `--server` and `session import URL`.
The TUI goes through the background service (the same session in the terminal and in the browser/phone). The service keeps
the configuration from the moment it started, so when a configuration file is newer, freecode tells it to reload
it (`opencode reload`, no restart; a restart only when the reload fails),
and if the service is busy with a session, it starts a private server (`--standalone`). `run` always goes
with `--standalone`. Auto-update and fetching the model list are disabled.

Variables: FREECODE_STANDALONE (TUI always with a private server),
FREECODE_HOSTS (extra allowed hosts, comma-separated, e.g. a server address in the LAN),
FREECODE_OPENCODE (path to opencode), FREECODE_URL + FREECODE_MODEL (configurator without questions),
FREECODE_YES (the configurator accepts the default answers).
Backward compatibility: --konfiguruj, --sprawdz, FREECODE_HOSTY and FREECODE_TAK also work.
"""
import ipaddress
import json
import os
import pwd
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

